(* RoboLang v2.2 grammar, implemented in src/robolang/parser.py.
   RoboLang 2.1 plus four more measured kinds and the unit "%" (decision 0044). Differences from grammar-v2.1.ebnf are
   marked [2.2]. Everything not shown here is as in grammar-v2.1.ebnf (and the grammars below it): a task and a mission are
   unchanged. Normative text, diagnostics and static rules: spec/robolang-v2.2.md *)

version_decl  = "robolang" , NUMBER , ";" ;                (* [2.2] NUMBER written exactly 2.2 *)

kind          = "temperature" | "mass" | "distance" | "label"
              | "fill" | "confidence" | "pressure" | "count" ;
                                                            (* [2.2] four more kinds. Kind names are recognised by their
                                                               position (after "measure r", before "of") and are NOT
                                                               reserved: "count" may still be a name. In a file below 2.2 the
                                                               four new ones are E217 *)

unit          = "degC" | "K" | "g" | "kg" | "mm" | "cm" | "m" | "ms" | "s" | "min"
              | "%"                                          (* [2.2] a token in 2.2 files only: fill and confidence, 0 to 100 *)
              | "Pa" | "kPa" | "bar" ;                       (* [2.2] pressure; 1 bar = 100 kPa, exactly *)

quantity      = NUMBER , unit ;                              (* [2.2] a count's literal has no unit: *)
operand       = quantity | NUMBER | STRING | NAME | TIME ;    (* [2.2] a bare NUMBER is a count's: a whole number, else E328.
                                                               Compared with any other kind it is E206 (the unit is required) *)

(* [2.2] The kinds and what each takes:
       measure r fill of shelf_a using cam as level;         if level < 25 % { ... }
       measure r confidence of plant_7 using cam as c;       if c >= 90 % { ... }
       measure r pressure of gauge_p3 using cam as p;        if p > 6 bar { ... }    (* also 600 kPa, 600000 Pa *)
       measure r count of shelf_3 using tag as n;            if n == 0 { ... }       (* also !=, <, <=, >, >= *)
   [2.2] The object of a "fill" or "count" reading is an object OR a location parameter ("bay 3 is empty"); of the other
   kinds, an object. A robot is not either: E304.
   [2.2] Units: "%" is a unit of two kinds, fill and confidence; the kind of the name decides. Comparing a fill with a
   confidence, or a count with a fill, is E319. A pressure converts exactly between Pa, kPa and bar. There is no "psi", no
   bare fraction (0.3) and no word "percent": the unit is required (E206). A count takes no unit (E319 if given) and no
   fraction (n < 2.5 is E328). A count is exact: it also takes "==" and "!="; the other kinds take "<", "<=", ">", ">=" only.
   [2.2] "confidence" is the sensor's confidence in the LABEL it reports for that object at that reading. To test "it is a weed
   and the detector is at least 90 percent sure", read both, "label" and "confidence", with the SAME sensor, and test both.
   Do not read confidence in place of the label, nor the label in place of the confidence.
   [2.2] Do not replace a reading: a request to read a fill, a pressure, a count or a confidence is never "inspect", never a
   "label" reading in its place, and never dropped. Either read it with the kind asked, or decline. Do not move a threshold or
   change its unit to avoid an unknown: 6 bar is 6 bar. Convert units only exactly (450 kPa is 4.5 bar).
   [2.2] Live readings work in a "wait until": wait until fill of tank_2 using cam > 80 % timeout 10 min else { ... }
   [2.2] What 2.2 cannot say (decision 0044 K8; erratum E5 of docs/evidence/bench-errata-1-8.md states why this belongs in
   the grammar file). Decline each as inexpressible:
   - a kind no sensor can declare: "how worn it looks", "whether it smells", "if the surface is clean". The four kinds are the
     whole list; the world cannot name a new kind;
   - a reading of a person: "how far the nearest person is". A person is not an object of the world;
   - a reading of the robot itself, such as its own battery: "measure r fill of r" is E304. G-SELF is a later version;
   - counting objects chosen at run time and acting on each: "carry as many cartons as counted". A plan has a fixed number of
     steps; a count is compared, never repeated;
   - arithmetic on readings: an average, a sum, a difference. Reading both is possible; computing is not.
   [2.2] "psi" is not a unit, and neither is a bare fraction or the word "percent": the author converts (as with degF), and
   writes "%", "bar", "kPa" or "Pa".
   [2.2] Meaning (spec/robolang-v2.2.md, spec/simulation-v1.0.md section 20): a reading is the property of the object or
   location now, in the sensor's unit, with the sensor's uncertainty (a count: none). An unknown comparison (a reading near the
   threshold) fails closed: an "if" fails the run, a "wait until" keeps waiting. A sensor must declare the kind in the world
   and be mounted on the robot; a request that names a sensor which does not measure the kind, or is on another robot, or is
   not available, with no other way to go on, is infeasible, not a program. *)

(* Reserved words in a 2.2 file: those of grammar-v2.1.ebnf. The kind names and "of" are not reserved. *)
